  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13960647",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-101022",
      "knowledge_point_id": "1916efa2-6a98-59a5-858c-c4fe64d8e8bc",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Magnitude of a Vector Given the Coordinates of its Endpoints",
      "problem": "Let $A(2,7,0)$ and $B(2,5,4).$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{AB}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=2i+7j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=2i+5j+4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =2i+5j+4k-(2i+7j) \\\\  & =-2j+4k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot ⟨0,-2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
